# Citation metadata for the SCORCH software (Citation File Format 1.2.0).
# The software release and the processed dataset are separate records:
#   software: this repository, version 1.0.0 (Zenodo software DOI to be added
#             once the GitHub release is archived; see docs/release_checklist.md)
#   dataset:  "Processed ERA5 daily maximum temperature data for SCORCH,
#             1940-2025", version 1.0.0, DOI 10.5281/zenodo.21717874
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use SCORCH, please cite this software release. When you use the
  study results, also cite the processed dataset (DOI 10.5281/zenodo.21717874)
  and the article once it is published.
type: software
title: "SCORCH: Spatiotemporal Classification of Regional Compound Heatwaves"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-10-11"
license: GPL-3.0-only
repository-code: "https://github.com/fawazbouhamad/compound-heatwave-typologies-SCORCH"
url: "https://github.com/fawazbouhamad/compound-heatwave-typologies-SCORCH"
abstract: >-
  SCORCH identifies regionally extensive compound heatwaves in daily maximum
  temperature fields. In five stages it detects local heatwaves at every grid
  cell, selects regionally extensive days, groups the heatwave cells of each
  day with DBSCAN, fits a PCA ellipse and a temperature-weighted centroid to
  every cluster, and joins consecutive days into compound events of four
  types. The repository holds the Python package and command-line tool, the
  R scripts of the power-law and log-Gaussian Cox process models, a
  synthetic demo, tests, and the catalogs, tables and figures of the study of
  the Eastern Mediterranean and Middle East (1940-2025).
keywords:
  - compound heatwaves
  - heatwave typology
  - spatiotemporal clustering
  - DBSCAN
  - PCA ellipses
  - log-Gaussian Cox process
  - ERA5 reanalysis
  - Eastern Mediterranean and Middle East
